    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d256">

                <pb facs="0565" n="529" xml:id="pg529"/>

                <head>256. Memorandum From Francis M.
                        Bator of the National Security Council Staff to President
                        Johnson<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d256-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1966-02-16T11:20:00-05:00">February 16, 1966, 11:20 a.m.</date>
                    </dateline></opener>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>SUBJECT</label>
                    <item>
                        Harold Wilson’s Message of this
                        Morning on the U.K. Defense Review</item>
                </list>
                <p>The important news about the British defense decisions—which is not in Harold Wilson’s message of this morning (Tab
                        A)<anchor n="2" xml:id="d256-f2"/>—is that they have decided to buy 50 of
                    our F-111’s, for delivery during ′68-′70. The total price will be $700-750
                    million, with payment over a 12-year period.</p>
                <p>Bob McNamara believes that, with the
                    F-111 order, our bilateral military balance-of-payments with the U.K. will be in the black—that our <hi
                        rendition="#italic">military</hi> dealings with the British will be on a
                    better than 100% offset basis.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d256-f3"/>
                </p>
                <p>Most important, the F-111 decision provides solid evidence that they have
                    committed themselves to a continuing and major East of Suez role.</p>
                <p>They are publishing their defense white paper on February 22. We will know more
                    about what is in it after McNamara’s
                    meeting with Healey tomorrow.<anchor
                        n="4" xml:id="d256-f4"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#bold">FMB</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d256-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d256-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Johnson Library, National Security File, Country File, UK, Vol. 2. Secret.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d256-f2" n="2" xml:id="d256-f2-content">
                <p>Not printed. In it Wilson stated that the
                    Cabinet had adopted a Defense White Paper for presentation to Parliament on
                    February 22 and that Defense Minister Healey would visit Washington to discuss the details of the plan
                    with Secretary McNamara.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d256-f3" n="3" xml:id="d256-f3-content">
                <p>On average over the next twelve years, with a reasonable year-by-year balance.
                        [Bator’s handwritten footnote in the
                    source text.]</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d256-f4" n="4" xml:id="d256-f4-content">
                <p>A handwritten note on the memorandum reads: “OK. L.”</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
